00:36:04.820 --> 00:36:08.166
towards the end as I started kind of losing it a little bit.

784
00:36:08.166 --> 00:36:10.490
And I simply needed to go into a room

785
00:36:10.490 --> 00:36:12.790
and lock the door and turn the AC on

786
00:36:12.790 --> 00:36:16.148
and turn some mindless television on.

787
00:36:16.148 --> 00:36:18.750
But for the most part at what I wanted to do

788
00:36:19.811 --> 00:36:20.644
was to put it out there

789
00:36:20.644 --> 00:36:23.840
that this is something that people can do

790
00:36:23.840 --> 00:36:25.590
and that there's alternatives

791
00:36:25.590 --> 00:36:28.780
to these to do in the Appalachian trail

792
00:36:28.780 --> 00:36:30.520
or go into Europe for four months.

793
00:36:30.520 --> 00:36:32.820
Like you can just pack a very light backpack

794
00:36:32.820 --> 00:36:34.880
and see what's out there.

795
00:36:34.880 --> 00:36:39.880
And it's really incredible when people see that all you have

796
00:36:42.330 --> 00:36:47.330
is in your backpack and they ask you if you've got a gun

797
00:36:47.340 --> 00:36:51.563
to protect yourself and like, no I don't have a gun.

798
00:36:53.182 --> 00:36:57.570
To just putting yourself out there in a vulnerable position.

799
00:36:57.570 --> 00:37:02.000
The amount of warmth and generosity that I experienced

800
00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:05.603
was something that I never could have anticipated

801
00:37:05.603 --> 00:37:06.727
in a million years.

802
00:37:06.727 --> 00:37:08.966
People giving me every last dollar from their wallet.

803
00:37:08.966 --> 00:37:11.403
There's the guy, what's his name?

804
00:37:12.388 --> 00:37:13.490
James Steel, yeah.

805
00:37:13.490 --> 00:37:15.551
Gives you 80 bucks or 160--

806
00:37:15.551 --> 00:37:16.384
I said 180.

807
00:37:16.384 --> 00:37:18.383
I said 80, it was 160.

808
00:37:18.383 --> 00:37:20.990
It was $160 and...

809
00:37:20.990 --> 00:37:23.123
He wasn't taking no for an answer.

810
00:37:23.991 --> 00:37:24.824
No, it was amazing.

811
00:37:24.824 --> 00:37:29.380
And that's when I kind of, I realized that people

812
00:37:30.670 --> 00:37:35.670
in their own way want to participate in this thing.

813
00:37:37.460 --> 00:37:39.440
A few years ago, so this is kind of something

814
00:37:39.440 --> 00:37:41.069
that I think about.

815
00:37:41.069 --> 00:37:43.333
And the best way for me to tell this

816
00:37:43.333 --> 00:37:47.751
is to talk about someone else running across the country.

817
00:37:47.751 --> 00:37:50.560
And that was Pete Kostelnik who did across the country

818
00:37:50.560 --> 00:37:54.240
in 40, I'm gonna say 44 days,

819
00:37:54.240 --> 00:37:56.130
it could have been a little quicker.

820
00:37:56.130 --> 00:37:59.920
This is in 2016, so a year before

821
00:37:59.920 --> 00:38:01.963
or a few months before I did mine.

822
00:38:03.050 --> 00:38:06.920
He did this and he was doing 60 to 70 miles a day

823
00:38:06.920 --> 00:38:10.790
for 45 days and broke the record that had stood for--

824
00:38:10.790 --> 00:38:12.428
Unsupported.

825
00:38:12.428 --> 00:38:13.300
That was very supported.

826
00:38:13.300 --> 00:38:15.980
That was two or three RVs with several people.

827
00:38:15.980 --> 00:38:19.760
Yeah, it was a big effort on for a lot of people

828
00:38:19.760 --> 00:38:22.813
and he's sure to give them credit as well.

829
00:38:24.189 --> 00:38:28.017
But for me personally, so I was in Wisconsin at the time

830
00:38:28.017 --> 00:38:30.800
and I saw that he was going through Northern Illinois

831
00:38:30.800 --> 00:38:34.030
and I got in my car and drove two and a half hours

832
00:38:34.030 --> 00:38:35.810
just to run with him for few miles.

833
00:38:35.810 --> 00:38:39.400
'Cause it's like, I just felt like it was seeing

834
00:38:39.400 --> 00:38:41.400
this mythical creature when someone's doing

835
00:38:41.400 --> 00:38:42.750
something like that.

836
00:38:42.750 --> 00:38:45.550
And even now to this day, now that I've done

837
00:38:45.550 --> 00:38:50.200
this big journey, I still think it's a mythical creature.

838
00:38:50.200 --> 00:38:52.710
It's like seeing a mountain lion or something.

839
00:38:52.710 --> 00:38:56.550
And so I think that for me, that's what it was

840
00:38:56.550 --> 00:38:58.740
for kind of when I came to realize

841
00:38:58.740 --> 00:39:01.050
that some people, when they wanted to give me money

842
00:39:01.050 --> 00:39:05.209
or just stop and talk, it was like, it is something rare.

843
00:39:05.209 --> 00:39:08.490
There's the one guy who it looks like he turned his car

844
00:39:08.490 --> 00:39:10.280
around when he saw you and he got out

845
00:39:10.280 --> 00:39:12.090
and he's like, my friend's gonna freak out.

846
00:39:12.090 --> 00:39:12.923
I read about you.

847
00:39:12.923 --> 00:39:15.180
Like when you cross the state line or something like that.

848
00:39:15.180 --> 00:39:17.970
So there was some awareness as you were passing through

849
00:39:17.970 --> 00:39:19.200
of what you were doing.

850
00:39:19.200 --> 00:39:21.340
Totally, in certain areas.

851
00:39:21.340 --> 00:39:23.080
I don't know why it was in certain areas.

852
00:39:23.080 --> 00:39:25.320
It was Oklahoma and Arkansas

853
00:39:26.309 --> 00:39:29.209
where I received the most amount of generosity and warmth.

854
00:39:30.340 --> 00:39:33.030
And then when I got to California,

855
00:39:33.030 --> 00:39:34.853
ironically, it was nothing.

856
00:39:36.740 --> 00:39:38.082
It's interesting like that.

857
00:39:38.082 --> 00:39:39.990
I think what I appreciate most

858
00:39:39.990 --> 00:39:44.220
is there's a vein of humility and vulnerability

859
00:39:44.220 --> 00:39:47.750
that infuses this effort and the other things

860
00:39:47.750 --> 00:39:48.820
that you've done.

861
00:39:48.820 --> 00:39:52.368
And this trans-Americana journey

862
00:39:52.368 --> 00:39:57.368
is almost like this performance art piece

863
00:40:00.060 --> 00:40:04.300
that is part de Tocqueville, part Henry David Thoreau.

864
00:40:04.300 --> 00:40:06.220
Like I'm gonna light out on America

865
00:40:06.220 --> 00:40:09.700
and learn about democracy and connect with people

866
00:40:09.700 --> 00:40:11.530
to try to better understand them,

867
00:40:11.530 --> 00:40:15.200
better understand myself and better understand

868
00:40:15.200 --> 00:40:18.730
what is required to unite us and bring us together.

869
00:40:18.730 --> 00:40:20.365
Totally.

870
00:40:20.365 --> 00:40:21.480
And you took your time,

871
00:40:21.480 --> 00:40:23.600
like the priority wasn't the running,

872
00:40:23.600 --> 00:40:25.290
the priority was the connection.

873
00:40:25.290 --> 00:40:26.430
Yeah.

874
00:40:26.430 --> 00:40:30.160
And the funniest thing that I encountered

875
00:40:30.160 --> 00:40:32.560
or the most interesting thing that I encountered

876
00:40:33.564 --> 00:40:34.397
when I went across the country

877
00:40:34.397 --> 00:40:38.550
is that I thought I would be talking politics all the time

878
00:40:38.550 --> 00:40:40.653
and it never came up.

879
00:40:41.996 --> 00:40:46.130
It was just like when you're doing something like that,

880
00:40:46.130 --> 00:40:48.100
when people see something like that,

881
00:40:48.100 --> 00:40:50.630
they don't wanna ask you what your political views are,

882
00:40:50.630 --> 00:40:53.442
they wanna ask you about like what you're doing

883
00:40:53.442 --> 00:40:54.275
and what you're seeing out there.

884
00:40:54.275 --> 00:40:57.270
And it just becomes really incredible

885
00:40:57.270 --> 00:40:59.886
that so much of this stuff just kind of dissolves away.

886
00:40:59.886 --> 00:41:02.877
And you realize that...

887
00:41:02.877 --> 00:41:06.741
And I think I say the same thing in the movie coming out

888
00:41:06.741 --> 00:41:10.010
is that I think, I don't think so, I know so.

889
00:41:10.010 --> 00:41:12.320
We're way more similar than we are different.

890
00:41:12.320 --> 00:41:15.339
I think that we're 90% similar and 10% different.

891
00:41:15.339 --> 00:41:20.217
And that 10% difference has become inflated

892
00:41:20.217 --> 00:41:23.390
so that we think that it's 90%, but it's not.

893
00:41:23.390 --> 00:41:24.760
Right, it's exploited.

894
00:41:24.760 --> 00:41:27.900
And it's leveraged by the media to further divide us.

895
00:41:27.900 --> 00:41:29.170
Totally.

896
00:41:29.170 --> 00:41:32.340
And I subscribe to my own media

897
00:41:32.340 --> 00:41:35.730
and a lot that's been brought to my attention

898
00:41:35.730 --> 00:41:38.670
over the past couple of years with talk of fake news

899
00:41:38.670 --> 00:41:41.610
and all of these things is coming to terms

900
00:41:41.610 --> 00:41:45.580
with that the media that I pay attention to is also biased.

901
00:41:45.580 --> 00:41:48.930
And it's not just Fox news, like NPR is also biased.

902
00:41:48.930 --> 00:41:50.680
We've all got these biases.

903
00:41:50.680 --> 00:41:54.930
And we like to think that we're right

904
00:41:54.930 --> 00:41:58.950
about our convictions, but the reality

905
00:41:58.950 --> 00:42:02.531
is that there's a million different paths out there.

906
00:42:02.531 --> 00:42:07.410
And if you grew up in Kansas, on a farm in Kansas

907
00:42:07.410 --> 00:42:09.610
and you had that lifestyle

908
00:42:09.610 --> 00:42:14.610
and I just see their voting habits,

909
00:42:16.560 --> 00:42:19.720
their convictions as every bit as valid as mine.

910
00:42:19.720 --> 00:42:23.560
And that's probably the biggest thing that I gained

911
00:42:23.560 --> 00:42:25.230
from my run across the countries

912
00:42:25.230 --> 00:42:27.510
is coming to terms with that.

913
00:42:27.510 --> 00:42:28.343
Yeah.

914
00:42:29.680 --> 00:42:31.300
So let's talk about Beauty Counter.

915
00:42:31.300 --> 00:42:34.777
I mean, walk me through the inspiration for this

916
00:42:34.777 --> 00:42:36.743 line:15%
and what gave you this idea?

917
00:42:38.310 --> 00:42:40.360 line:15%
You know, there wasn't one specific thing,

918
00:42:40.360 --> 00:42:43.230 line:15%
but it was actually last night my friend Lila Rose

919
00:42:43.230 --> 00:42:44.620 line:15%
is in town from New York visiting.

920
00:42:44.620 --> 00:42:48.340
And it was Lila, Lila had watched an Inconvenient Truth

921
00:42:48.340 --> 00:42:50.920
and became super passionate

922
00:42:50.920 --> 00:42:53.437
about the environmental health movement and said to me,

923
00:42:53.437 --> 00:42:55.406
"You need to watch this film.

924
00:42:55.406 --> 00:43:00.037
"You're loud, you're direct, you are connected.

925
00:43:00.037 --> 00:43:01.287
"And I think you should pay attention

926
00:43:01.287 --> 00:43:03.091
"to what's going on with the environment."

927
00:43:03.091 --> 00:43:04.722
And so I watched "An Inconvenient Truth"

928
00:43:04.722 --> 00:43:07.190
and for whatever reason that movie rocked my world.

929
00:43:07.190 --> 00:43:08.610
It was just the first time,

930
00:43:08.610 --> 00:43:10.370
I know it's almost embarrassing to admit this,

931
00:43:10.370 --> 00:43:12.800
but it was the first time that I truly paid attention

932
00:43:12.800 --> 00:43:15.290
to the fact that things that I was doing,

933
00:43:15.290 --> 00:43:18.440
that were doing in my daily life, that my very existence

934
00:43:18.440 --> 00:43:21.840
was wreaking havoc on the earth.

935
00:43:21.840 --> 00:43:25.600
And so I became focused on the environmental health movement

936
00:43:25.600 --> 00:43:28.047
and started to really make changes in my life.

937
00:43:28.047 --> 00:43:30.640
Over the next year or two,

938
00:43:30.640 --> 00:43:34.060
I also had a situation where I had a woman

939
00:43:34.060 --> 00:43:36.400
who was taking care of our kids or Phoebe at the time,

940
00:43:36.400 --> 00:43:38.330
my oldest, who was our nanny.

941
00:43:38.330 --> 00:43:40.410
I was working, full-time running best in company.

942
00:43:40.410 --> 00:43:44.060
And at 31, she was diagnosed with a non HPV related

943
00:43:44.060 --> 00:43:46.832
cervical cancer and within 11 months she had died.

944
00:43:46.832 --> 00:43:50.555
And so I was watching this young, beautiful

945
00:43:50.555 --> 00:43:52.420
amazing woman die in front of me.

946
00:43:52.420 --> 00:43:53.870
I was watching all these friends of mine

947
00:43:53.870 --> 00:43:56.100
struggle with fertility issues or giving birth

948
00:43:56.100 --> 00:43:58.662
to kids who had pretty significant health issues.

949
00:43:58.662 --> 00:44:01.617
I looked at what was happening in the environment.

950
00:44:01.617 --> 00:44:02.450
I thought like something's gone terribly right

951
00:44:02.450 --> 00:44:05.150
and maybe I need to do something about it.

952
00:44:05.150 --> 00:44:07.677
And it wasn't about beauty or Beauty Counter at the time.

953
00:44:07.677 --> 00:44:10.710
It was just, how do I start to become,

954
00:44:10.710 --> 00:44:12.410
how do I educate myself on what's actually going on?

955
00:44:12.410 --> 00:44:15.690
That's there so that can be a vocal point for change.

956
00:44:15.690 --> 00:44:18.420
And there's kind of this epiphany moment

957
00:44:18.420 --> 00:44:20.880
when you're using products on your kids,

958
00:44:20.880 --> 00:44:23.850
thinking 'cause they have the words natural on them

959
00:44:23.850 --> 00:44:26.560
or you think you're using the right brand or whatever.

960
00:44:26.560 --> 00:44:28.560
And then looking at the ingredients label

961
00:44:28.560 --> 00:44:30.221
and doing a little research and realizing

962
00:44:30.221 --> 00:44:35.090
that the gap between what you're being told or sold

963
00:44:35.090 --> 00:44:37.283
and the truth is pretty vast.

964
00:44:38.320 --> 00:44:41.500
I thought I was that has that whole foods shopper.

965
00:44:41.500 --> 00:44:43.690
I was the mom that was going to local farm stands

966
00:44:43.690 --> 00:44:44.530
as eating organic.

967
00:44:44.530 --> 00:44:46.985
I mean, I thought I was doing all the right things.

968
00:44:46.985 --> 00:44:47.818
I gotten rid of all our plastic.

969
00:44:47.818 --> 00:44:49.620
I was washing my floors with water and vinegar.

970
00:44:49.620 --> 00:44:53.403
And then I was looking at this and my kids,

971
00:44:53.403 --> 00:44:54.910
I had two kids in the tub and it was a natural foaming

972
00:44:54.910 --> 00:44:57.610
oatmeal bodywash from a leading drugstore brand.

973
00:44:57.610 --> 00:44:59.280
And I thought, I mean, it looks like oatmeal,

974
00:44:59.280 --> 00:45:00.580
it smelled like oatmeal kind of to me.

975
00:45:00.580 --> 00:45:01.860
And then someone told me

976
00:45:01.860 --> 00:45:04.090
about the environmental working group, skin deep database.

977
00:45:04.090 --> 00:45:05.370
And I took a look at it and I was like,

978
00:45:05.370 --> 00:45:07.620
holy shit, it was like eight out of nine for toxicity.

979
00:45:07.620 --> 00:45:09.410
And I was pretty outraged.

980
00:45:09.410 --> 00:45:11.819
So that really was another trigger point for me.

981
00:45:11.819 --> 00:45:14.747
That's when I started switching all of my family's products.

982
00:45:14.747 --> 00:45:18.332
But the one thing that I couldn't find for myself was just

983
00:45:18.332 --> 00:45:21.880
I couldn't find any skincare cosmetics that I wanted to use.

984
00:45:21.880 --> 00:45:24.860
And there just wasn't anything out there.

985
00:45:24.860 --> 00:45:27.180
And the more that I learned, the more that I realized

986
00:45:27.180 --> 00:45:29.650
that we had laws that were incredibly outdated,

987
00:45:29.650 --> 00:45:31.240
the more I realized that there were harmful chemicals

988
00:45:31.240 --> 00:45:33.610
in our products, I wanted to do something about it,

989
00:45:33.610 --> 00:45:35.333
which is really why I started the company.

990
00:45:36.552 --> 00:45:37.840
I consider myself to be somebody

991
00:45:37.840 --> 00:45:41.220
who's fairly up to speed on environmental issues.

992
00:45:41.220 --> 00:45:43.330
And certainly I'm the first to say

993
00:45:43.330 --> 00:45:46.029
that governmental regulatory bodies

994
00:45:46.029 --> 00:45:49.880
are not necessarily looking out for your best interests,

995
00:45:49.880 --> 00:45:52.720
but even I was shocked when we were talking.

996
00:45:52.720 --> 00:45:54.541
You were telling me last year

997
00:45:54.541 --> 00:45:59.541
about the extent to which the FDA is just this paper tiger

998
00:46:00.870 --> 00:46:04.730
when it comes to protecting people on the cosmetic

999
00:46:04.730 --> 00:46:06.950
and kind of skincare front.

1000
00:46:06.950 --> 00:46:07.783
Yeah, I mean, I think that...

1001
00:46:07.783 --> 00:46:12.300
Someone was asking me the other day, why people worry,

1002
00:46:12.300 --> 00:46:15.340
why they feel safe.

1003
00:46:15.340 --> 00:46:17.110
Why do people feel safe in the United States

1004
00:46:17.110 --> 00:46:19.400
in terms of the products on the shelves.

1005
00:46:19.400 --> 00:46:22.490
And I was saying that in the food industry,

1006
00:46:22.490 --> 00:46:24.130
there are two things I think that are different

1007
00:46:24.130 --> 00:46:25.745
about skincare and cosmetics.

1008
00:46:25.745 --> 00:46:28.100
And just for clarity purposes,

1009
00:46:28.100 --> 00:46:31.210
I know you have a lot of men that listen to you

1010
00:46:31.210 --> 00:46:32.290
talk about things.

1011
00:46:32.290 --> 00:46:35.890
This, isn't red lipstick, this is deodorant, sunscreen,

1012
00:46:35.890 --> 00:46:37.820
body lotion, anything that we're putting on our bodies,

1013
00:46:37.820 --> 00:46:38.653
any of those things.

1014
00:46:38.653 --> 00:46:40.067
I think that two things are different.

1015
00:46:40.067 --> 00:46:43.040
When you eat something that's really bad for your body,

1016
00:46:43.040 --> 00:46:44.260
you typically get sick.

1017
00:46:44.260 --> 00:46:45.340
Like right away.

1018
00:46:45.340 --> 00:46:46.320
You feel it whereas--

1019
00:46:46.320 --> 00:46:47.830
There's an immediate direct reaction.

1020
00:46:47.830 --> 00:46:50.170
Whereas if you're putting on sunscreen or body lotion,

1021
00:46:50.170 --> 00:46:51.380
you could go your whole life and you would never

1022
00:46:51.380 --> 00:46:53.610
necessarily know that it was harmful to your health.

1023
00:46:53.610 --> 00:46:55.090
And then the second thing is,

1024
00:46:55.090 --> 00:46:56.760
I mean, yes if you have an allergic reaction,

1025
00:46:56.760 --> 00:46:58.518
but in the absence of allergic reactions,

1026
00:46:58.518 --> 00:47:01.240
people would just continue to put stuff on their body.

1027
00:47:01.240 --> 00:47:02.073
They wouldn't know about it.

1028
00:47:02.073 --> 00:47:03.770
And I think the second thing that's different

1029
00:47:03.770 --> 00:47:06.750
is that the FDA in the food industry has the ability

1030
00:47:06.750 --> 00:47:08.820
to recall product when it's known to be harmful to health.

1031
00:47:08.820 --> 00:47:11.120
If there's a seminal outbreak or whatever it is,

1032
00:47:11.120 --> 00:47:12.570
they can immediately pull it from the shelf.

1033
00:47:12.570 --> 00:47:14.490
But what people don't realize in the United States

1034
00:47:14.490 --> 00:47:16.840
is that when it comes to skincare and cosmetics,

1035
00:47:16.840 --> 00:47:18.830
they do not have the ability to recall product.

1036
00:47:18.830 --> 00:47:20.960
They can suggest, but they cannot take action.

1037
00:47:20.960 --> 00:47:23.310
That is shocking to me.

1038
00:47:23.310 --> 00:47:24.920
So play that out.

